# The Cornell box, from the measured data
#
# Geometry, camera and light source are those published by the Cornell Program of Computer
# Graphics: https://www.graphics.cornell.edu/online/box/data.html — the box as it was built and
# measured, not the idealised version `cornell_box_canonical.stage` draws.
#
# Run it with
#
#   --fov 39.3077 --far 2000 --output_width 512 --output_height 512
#   --integrator naive --max_depth 8 --samples_ppx 4000
#
# `--fov` is the aperture of the measured camera: a 0.025 film behind a 0.035 lens subtends
# 2·atan(0.0125 / 0.035) = 39.3077°. The film is square, so the image must be square too — on a
# non-square image the renderer fits the field of view to the shorter side and the framing is no
# longer the measured one. `--far` must clear the distance from the eye to the back wall,
# 800 + 559.2 = 1359.2, which the default of 1000 does not.
#
# That field of view is a little wider than the opening it looks into: 571.4 across at the plane
# z = 0, against a room 556 wide and 548.8 high. The thin black margin around the image is
# therefore the measured framing itself, and cropping it would mean moving the camera or narrowing
# the lens.
#
# The room is white and closed, so most of what lights it has bounced: measured against a render
# at depth 12, a wall is 1% short at `--max_depth 8`, 2 to 4% short at 6 and 8 to 13% short at 4.
# Depth is therefore not where to save time here — sample count is, and `naive` needs a great many
# of them, having no way to aim a ray at the panel.
#
# `--integrator naive` is not a taste: this scene is lit by an emissive surface and by nothing
# else, which is what the measured box is. `path` samples `Scene::lights` — the declared point and
# infinite lights — and adds surface emission only after a specular bounce, so it would draw the
# panel on a black room. `naive` gathers emission at every hit, which is the whole scene here.
# The comparison of the two is what `AreaLight` has to close; see `ideas/area_light.md`.
#
#
# FRAME — z is negated with respect to the published table
#
# The Cornell data is authored for a right-handed camera: eye at (278, 273, -800) looking along
# +z, up +y, which puts the red wall (x ≈ 556) on the left of the image. `Matrix4::look_at` builds
# `right = up × dir`, a left-handed basis — pbrt's convention — so feeding it those coordinates
# verbatim would mirror the image and hang the red wall on the right.
#
# So every z of the table is negated here, and the eye moves to +z. x and y are the published
# numbers untouched; a z is the published number with a minus sign. The room therefore occupies
#
#   x ∈ [0, 556]      y ∈ [0, 548.8]      z ∈ [-559.2, 0]
#
# and is open towards the camera, at z = 0. A mirror reverses the sense of a rotation, which is
# why the two blocks turn the opposite way from the angles one reads off the published corners.
#
#
# WHAT THIS FILE CANNOT SAY EXACTLY
#
# The measured surfaces are quadrilaterals, and the `.stage` language has rectangles and boxes.
# Four of the five come through intact: the ceiling and the green wall are exact rectangles in the
# data, and the floor and the back wall lie exactly in their measured planes — only their edge on
# the red side is carried out from the measured 549.6 and 552.8 to 556, into the corner where the
# red wall hides it.
# The red wall itself is the one real departure: the data gives a leaning quad, from x = 552.8 and
# 549.6 at the floor to x = 556 at the ceiling, and it stands here as the vertical plane x = 556,
# up to 6.4 away from the measurement at the floor line.
#
# The blocks are measured quads too, their corners out of square by up to a degree and their sides
# a unit or two apart. Each is reproduced by the box that fits it best: side = mean of the two
# opposite measured edges, angle = mean of the four edge directions. Every corner then lands within
# 1.1 of the published one, which the comments below record block by block.
#
# The reflectances and the emission are the RGB triples this scene is usually given, not the
# measured spectra: the data is spectral, from 400 to 700 nm, and this renderer carries three
# components. What survives of the measurement is its shape — a light that rises towards the red,
# hence markedly warm, and a red wall far more saturated than the green one.
#
# A `transform` block composes its steps as `translation · rotation`, so the rotation is listed
# first and turns the shape about its own centre before the translation carries it into place.

camera pin_hole
  pos 278.0 273.0 800.0
  look 278.0 273.0 0.0
  up 0.0 1.0 0.0

scene

  # Floor, y = 0. The reference orientation of `rectangle` already faces up, into the room.
  object transformed
    object simple
      rectangle 556.0 559.2
      lambertian color 0.725 0.71 0.68
    transform {
      translate 278.0 0.0 -279.6
    }

  # Ceiling, y = 548.8. Flipped by a half turn about x so its normal faces down: a lambertian
  # scatters into the hemisphere of its normal, so a wall facing away from the room is a black
  # wall.
  object transformed
    object simple
      rectangle 556.0 559.2
      lambertian color 0.725 0.71 0.68
    transform {
      rotate_x 3.14159265
      translate 278.0 548.8 -279.6
    }

  # Back wall, z = -559.2. A quarter turn about x stands the rectangle up and aims its normal at
  # +z; the 548.8 of the pair is then the room's height.
  object transformed
    object simple
      rectangle 556.0 548.8
      lambertian color 0.725 0.71 0.68
    transform {
      rotate_x 1.57079633
      translate 278.0 274.4 -559.2
    }

  # Right wall, green, x = 0. An exact rectangle in the data. A quarter turn about z aims the
  # normal at +x, and the first side of the pair becomes the height.
  object transformed
    object simple
      rectangle 548.8 559.2
      lambertian color 0.14 0.45 0.091
    transform {
      rotate_z -1.57079633
      translate 0.0 274.4 -279.6
    }

  # Left wall, red, x = 556. The leaning quad of the data, stood upright; see the header.
  object transformed
    object simple
      rectangle 548.8 559.2
      lambertian color 0.63 0.065 0.05
    transform {
      rotate_z 1.57079633
      translate 556.0 274.4 -279.6
    }

  # Short block, at the green wall, turned 16.96° = 0.2960 rad. The measured top face is
  # (130, 65) (82, 225) (240, 272) (290, 114) in the published frame: sides 167.0, 164.8, 165.7,
  # 167.3, corners out of square by up to 1.0°. The box below reproduces those corners to 1.1.
  object transformed
    object simple
      aabox 166.1 165.0 166.4
      lambertian color 0.725 0.71 0.68
    transform {
      rotate_y 0.2960
      translate 185.5 82.5 -169.0
    }

  # Tall block, at the red wall, turned -17.24° = -0.3009 rad. Measured top face
  # (423, 247) (265, 296) (314, 456) (472, 406): sides 165.4, 167.3, 165.7, 166.4, corners out of
  # square by up to 0.5°, reproduced to 0.6.
  object transformed
    object simple
      aabox 165.6 330.0 166.9
      lambertian color 0.725 0.71 0.68
    transform {
      rotate_y -0.3009
      translate 368.5 165.0 -351.25
    }

  # The light: the 130 × 105 panel of the data, x ∈ [213, 343], z ∈ [-332, -227]. The data sets it
  # in the ceiling plane, y = 548.8; it hangs a tenth below so that the two surfaces are not
  # coplanar and no ray has to arbitrate between them. Turned a half turn about x to aim it down:
  # emission is one-sided, so the normal decides which way the lamp points.
  object transformed
    object simple
      rectangle 130.0 105.0
      diffuse_light color 18.4 15.6 8.0
    transform {
      rotate_x 3.14159265
      translate 278.0 548.7 -279.5
    }
